course: dictionary
lesson: 8806
title: A name or an old word?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one fits. Look at the clues around the word. «Shay lives in a children's home in Dublin with five other kids and the staff.» Shay starts with a capital letter and is a person who lives somewhere, so it is a first name.
explain: Ask yourself who or what is doing the action. «Shay ran 3 km.» A person runs, so Shay is a character's name here. Names of people are not usually in the dictionary as everyday words, but this one is, so check its meanings.
explain: Shay can also be an old word. In the poem «The Deacon's Masterpiece, or the Wonderful One-Hoss Shay», the clues are horses, wheels and an old-fashioned story, so a shay must be a carriage. A dictionary confirms it.
fact: Shay is a first name for boys and girls.
fact: Shay comes from the Irish names Séamus or Séaghdha.
fact: Shay is also a surname, which is a family name.
fact: A shay is an old-fashioned light, two-wheeled carriage pulled by a horse.
fact: The word shay is made from «chaise».
exercise: «Shay lives in a children's home in Dublin.» In this sentence, what is Shay? | A street in Dublin | *A person's first name | A horse pulling a carriage | A kind of school bag
exercise: In an old poem, «a shay» is most likely what? | A kind of hat | *A light, two-wheeled carriage pulled by a horse | A friend's pet dog | A light, two-wheeled carriage
exercise: Which fact about Shay is true? | It means very old | It is only used for horses | *It is a first name used for boys and girls | It is always a town in Ireland
test: «Shay ran 3 km.» In this sentence, Shay is most likely | A spelling mistake | A carriage | A type of horse | *A character's name
test: The word «shay» is made from which older word? | *chaise | chapel | shore | shadow
exam: Which clue shows that «shay» means a carriage, not a name? | A boy called Shay in a children's home | A sentence that starts with Shay | Shay is used in a maths problem about running | *A poem about a horse-drawn one-hoss shay
